D={
"yuan-dynasty":dict(government="Kublai Khan ruled China as Great Khan and Emperor, dividing society into four ranks: Mongols, Semu (Central Asians and others), northern Chinese and southern Chinese. The examination system was suspended until 1315 and many officials were foreigners, among them Persians, Uyghurs and, by his own account, Marco Polo.",architecture="The new capital Dadu (Khanbaliq, today's Beijing) was laid out on a classical grid with palaces, lakes and the White Pagoda, built by the Nepali architect Araniko.",technology="Paper money circulated empire-wide. The Grand Canal was extended to Beijing. Guo Shoujing built observatories and calculated the year to within 26 seconds. Postal relay stations (yam) linked China to Persia.",rise="Genghis Khan's Mongols attacked the Jin from 1211. His grandson Kublai declared the Yuan dynasty in 1271 and destroyed the Southern Song in 1279, the first time a non-Chinese people ruled all of China.",fall="Failed invasions of Japan (1274, 1281) and Java drained the treasury, and paper-money inflation and Yellow River floods followed. Plague and ethnic discrimination fuelled the Red Turban revolts. Zhu Yuanzhang took Dadu in 1368 and the Mongols withdrew to the steppe.",legacy="It reunified China after centuries of division and opened the age of Silk Road contact with Europe described by Marco Polo. It also produced zaju drama."),
"goguryeo-baekje-and-silla":dict(government="In the Three Kingdoms period, Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla were monarchies ruled with powerful aristocracies. Silla used the 'bone-rank' system: birth into 'sacred bone' or 'true bone' lineages decided who could hold office.",architecture="Goguryeo left painted tombs and mountain fortresses. Baekje's Mireuksa temple pagoda and Silla's capital Gyeongju (Cheomseongdae observatory, Bulguksa temple, Seokguram grotto) survive.",technology="Silla's Cheomseongdae (7th century) is among the oldest surviving observatories in East Asia. Baekje transmitted writing, Buddhism and craftsmen to Japan. All three produced superb gold crowns and metalwork.",rise="The kingdoms took shape from the 1st century BCE to the 4th century CE. Goguryeo grew to rule much of Manchuria under Gwanggaeto the Great (r. 391–413).",fall="Silla allied with Tang China to conquer Baekje (660) and Goguryeo (668), then expelled the Tang by 676 to unify most of the peninsula. Unified Silla later split amid noble revolts and gave way to Goryeo in 935.",legacy="The first unification of Korea, Korean Buddhist art and the transmission of continental culture to Japan."),
"joseon-korea":dict(government="Joseon was a Neo-Confucian monarchy run by scholar-officials (yangban) chosen by civil service examinations. Royal records (the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty) were kept with such independence that even kings could not read them.",architecture="Gyeongbokgung and Changdeokgung palaces in Seoul, Jongmyo royal shrine and the Hwaseong fortress at Suwon.",technology="King Sejong (r. 1418–1450) commissioned Hangul (1443), a phonetic alphabet designed so ordinary people could read. His scholars also built rain gauges, water clocks and sundials. Admiral Yi Sun-sin's ironclad 'turtle ships' defeated Japanese fleets in the 1590s.",rise="General Yi Seong-gye overthrew the Goryeo dynasty in 1392 and moved the capital to Hanyang (Seoul).",fall="Japanese (1592–98) and Manchu (1627, 1636) invasions devastated the country. Factional strife and isolation as the 'Hermit Kingdom' followed. Japan forced open its ports (1876), made Korea a protectorate in 1905 and annexed it in 1910.",legacy="Hangul, Confucian family culture and one of the world's longest-ruling dynasties (518 years)."),
"heian-japan":dict(government="The emperor reigned in Heian-kyō (Kyoto), but real power passed to the Fujiwara clan, who ruled as regents by marrying daughters into the imperial family. Later, retired emperors ruled 'from the cloister' (insei).",architecture="Kyoto was laid out on a grid modelled on Chang'an. Byōdō-in's Phoenix Hall and shinden-style aristocratic mansions with gardens and ponds also survive.",technology="The kana syllabaries developed, letting writers, many of them court women, write Japanese phonetically.",rise="Emperor Kanmu moved the capital to Heian-kyō in 794 to escape the power of Nara's Buddhist monasteries.",fall="Provincial warrior clans grew strong. The Genpei War (1180–85) between the Taira and Minamoto ended with Minamoto no Yoritomo founding the Kamakura shogunate in 1185.",legacy="Murasaki Shikibu's The Tale of Genji (~1010), often called the world's first novel, and Sei Shōnagon's Pillow Book."),
"edo-japan-tokugawa":dict(government="The shogun ruled from Edo, while about 260 daimyo governed their domains. The 'alternate attendance' system (sankin-kōtai) made daimyo spend every other year in Edo and leave families there as hostages. Society was ranked as samurai, farmers, artisans and merchants.",architecture="Edo Castle, Himeji Castle (completed 1609), Nikkō's Tōshō-gū shrine and merchant townhouses.",technology="Japan was largely closed to foreigners (sakoku) except the Dutch at Dejima, through whom 'Dutch learning' (rangaku) brought European medicine and science. Literacy was high and woodblock printing (ukiyo-e) became a mass art.",rise="Tokugawa Ieyasu won the Battle of Sekigahara (1600) and became shogun in 1603, ending a century of civil war.",fall="Commodore Perry's US ships (1853) forced Japan open. Unequal treaties and domestic unrest followed, and samurai from Satsuma and Chōshū overthrew the shogunate in the Meiji Restoration (1868).",legacy="Two and a half centuries of peace, haiku (Bashō), kabuki, Hokusai's woodblock prints and the foundations of modern Japan."),
"funan":dict(government="Funan, known from Chinese records, was ruled by kings, said to descend from the marriage of an Indian Brahmin named Kaundinya and a local queen named Soma.",architecture="Óc Eo, its port in the Mekong delta, had canals linking it to inland cities, brick buildings and workshops.",technology="Canals for drainage and transport. Roman, Indian and Chinese goods have been found at Óc Eo.",rise="It grew from about the 1st century CE as a stop on the sea route between India and China.",fall="Shifts in shipping routes through the Strait of Malacca weakened it, and by the 7th century Chenla had absorbed it.",legacy="The earliest Indianised state of mainland Southeast Asia."),
"srivijaya":dict(government="A thalassocracy: maharajas at Palembang controlled the Strait of Malacca and Sunda Strait through allied ports and the loyalty of sea peoples (orang laut).",architecture="Little survives at Palembang. Related monuments include Muara Takus and, in Java, the Sailendra dynasty's Borobudur.",technology="Navigation with monsoon winds and control of shipping and taxes on the China–India sea trade.",rise="Srivijaya emerged around 650 CE in Sumatra. The Chinese monk Yijing (671) found over a thousand monks studying there.",fall="The Chola king Rajendra I raided its ports in 1025. Later, Javanese Singhasari and Majapahit took over its role.",legacy="A major centre of Buddhist learning; Old Malay inscriptions are the ancestors of Malay and Indonesian."),
"khmer-empire":dict(government="The god-king (devaraja) cult linked the Khmer king to Shiva or Vishnu. The king ruled a vast network of provinces through officials and temple foundations.",architecture="Angkor Wat (early 12th century, built by Suryavarman II) is the largest religious monument in the world. Angkor Thom, the faces of the Bayon, and Ta Prohm (built by Jayavarman VII) also survive.",technology="A huge hydraulic system of barays (reservoirs), canals and moats. Lidar shows Angkor was a sprawling low-density city of perhaps 750,000–900,000 people.",rise="Jayavarman II declared independence from Java and himself universal monarch in 802 at Phnom Kulen.",fall="Overstretched water infrastructure, alternating mega-droughts and floods in the 14th–15th centuries, a shift to Theravada Buddhism and maritime trade, and attacks by Ayutthaya (1431) led the capital to move south.",legacy="Angkor Wat is on Cambodia's flag. Khmer architecture influenced all of mainland Southeast Asia."),
"majapahit":dict(government="Kings ruled from Trowulan in East Java with powerful ministers, most famously Gajah Mada, who swore the Palapa oath not to rest until the archipelago was united.",architecture="Red-brick temples (candi) and gates such as Bajang Ratu, with the canals of Trowulan.",technology="Rice irrigation, a large fleet and the spice trade.",rise="Raden Wijaya used a Mongol invasion force to defeat his rival in 1293, then drove the Mongols out.",fall="Succession wars, the rise of Muslim port states such as Demak and Malacca, and Demak's conquest around 1527.",legacy="A symbol of Indonesian unity; the Nagarakretagama poem. Bali preserves its Hindu culture."),
"polynesian-voyagers":dict(government="Chiefs (ariki) of ranked lineages, tracing descent from gods and founding canoe voyagers, led island societies. Hawai'i and Tonga developed paramount kingships.",architecture="Marae temples, Tonga's Ha'amonga trilithon, Rapa Nui's moai statues and Hawai'i's heiau and fishponds.",technology="Double-hulled voyaging canoes navigated without instruments, using star paths, ocean swells, birds and clouds. The Hōkūleʻa voyages since 1976 proved such journeys possible.",rise="Lapita ancestors reached Tonga and Samoa by about 800 BCE. Their descendants settled East Polynesia, Hawai'i, Rapa Nui and finally Aotearoa (~1250 CE).",fall="European contact from the 18th century brought diseases that killed many, missionaries, plantations and colonial annexation.",legacy="The most widespread human migration before modern times, across a third of the globe's surface."),
}
